WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:01.660
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:03.540
So, you know the Obamacare insurance exchanges

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.460
that, depending on your political perspective,

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.840
are either the cause of or the solution to

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.780
so many of America's health care problems?

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.820
Only about six percent of Americans

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.360
actually get their insurance through those exchanges.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.900
Now, that's obviously very important to those six percent of people,

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.120
but it's by far the smallest slice

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:20.980
of the American health insurance pie.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.720
And I think the relentless focus on them in our political discourse

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.980
speaks to a larger inability to have,

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.400
for lack of a better phrase,

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.760
a grown-up conversation about health care.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.720
OK, so health care is very complicated

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.980
and I'm going to paint this in the broadest possible strokes.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.840
If you want far more information about health and health care,

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.160
please check out Healthcare Triage.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.000
But let's start with what health insurance does.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.720
Health insurance takes money from healthy people

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.860
and uses it to buy care for sick people.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.620
you can do this through private insurance,

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.160
which pools people together and takes money from them in the form of monthly premiums,

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.020
oror you can do it through a public insurer

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.980
which pools people together and takes money from them in the form of taxes.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:55.780
In the United States, we have both:

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.560
Almost everyone over the age of 65

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.340
is in this publicly funded insurance pool called Medicare.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:03.220
Many military veterans are in a similar pool run by the Veterans Administration

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:08.120
and there's a third public pool that covers many low-income and disabled Americans called Medicaid

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.460
but just over half of Americans get their insurance

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:11.940
through private insurers

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:15.140
This includes the six percent of people covered through the Obamacare exchanges,

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.700
but mostly people who get their insurance from their employers.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.680
Because large companies are required to offer

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.200
health insurance plans to their employees.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.960
Then, you have the nine percent of Americans

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.600
who don't have any health insurance at all.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.780
That number has dropped a lot in the last five years,

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.220
but it's still higher than any other wealthy nation's.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.500
So, the current U.S health care system is widely considered to be,

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.800
if I can use a technical term, bananas.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.300
We spend so, so much on health care.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.840
About eighteen percent of our total economic output

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.020
goes to health care.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.540
The average among other wealthy nations

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.060
is closer to twelve percent.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.900
And we also don't get particularly good health care outcomes.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.080
Like, we come up short in many, many metrics,

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.180
including life expectancy, where we trail countries like Chile and Slovenia,

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:56.380
both of which spend less than ten percent of their GDP on health care

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.640
and, for the record, both of which also have higher rates of tobacco usage.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.700
Now, you would think with lots of private participation

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:02.520
in health care markets,

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.240
costs would be driven down, right?

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.860
Like, private companies can make refrigerators or cars

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.320
much better than governments can make refrigerators or cars.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.440
But health care's a weird market on a few levels.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.400
First, there's the problem of inelastic demand.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.280
Like, if I need a medicine to keep me alive,

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.400
I can't effectively negotiate the price of that medication

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.060
because, in the end, I'm going to pay whatever it costs.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.380
And then there's the problem of competition being something of an illusion.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.340
Like, for instance, if you're having a heart attack,

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.420
it's generally not considered a good idea

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.420
to call around to a bunch of area hospitals

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.500
to find out who has the best deal on heart surgery this week.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.680
There should be competition among private insurers

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.940
but plans are often extremely confusing.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.520
Like, you might pay less up front and think you're getting a good deal,

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.940
only to find out later that what you need to be covered isn't covered.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:46.020
Like, it's hard to compare cell phone plans effectively,

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.540
let alone health insurance plans that might or might not cover

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.040
literally thousands of different procedures.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.720
Plus, having your insurance status depend partly on whether you work for a big company

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.040
is a huge disincentive to innovation and entrepreneurship.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:59.720
It tells people, "Don't start a new business,"

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:03.160
"Don't strike out on your own because you won't be able to get affordable health insurance."

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.200
So, all in all, we've ended up with a health care system

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.820
where more tax dollars per person go to publicly funded health care

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:11.060
than in most other countries

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.260
and yet we don't have publicly funded health care for most people.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.020
And most Americans agree on this:

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:19.540
only about thirty-two percent of us think that our health care system is good or excellent.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.140
And yet, around seventy percent of Americans

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.140
think that their personal health care

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:25.700
is good or excellent

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.240
and therein lies the problem.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.940
Most serious reform proposals

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.820
would involve big changes

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.100
for many people in that seventy percent.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.960
And, as the Republican party has lately learned,

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.320
it's easy to talk about health care reform in the abstract

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:38.680
but when you put pen to paper,

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.240
it gets complicated.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.820
So all health care reform boils down to three factors:

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.880
Quality, cost, and access.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.080
Obamacare sought to improve access:

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.260
It didn't do much to change quality or cost.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.260
And to do that, it spent a huge amount of money.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.340
Like, a trillion dollars in the first decade.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.340
Now, it increased some taxes and cut some spending,

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:56.740
so as to be deficit-neutral,

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:00.300
but it spent a lot of money to get a lot of people health insurance.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.140
So today, fewer people are uninsured,

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:03.820
but it cost a lot to get there:

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:04.680
That was the trade-off.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.680
The Republican bill sought to reduce government spending

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.700
on health care, but that also comes with trade-offs.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.600
In this case, it would have resulted in millions of people losing their insurance,

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.600
which was a hard sell to moderate Republicans,

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.000
so the GOP bill offered tax credits to help people buy private insurance,

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.240
but that meant more spending,

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.720
which made it a hard sell to conservative Republicans.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.000
Like the ACA itself, the GOP bill would not have done

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.360
a lot to address the overall cost of health care in the United States

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.580
or the fact that our quality of care isn't great.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:31.020
Because to have those conversations, we need to accept,

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:32.940
that, as Healthcare Triage always says,

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.700
"Tradeoffs are inevitable."

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:37.020
Side note: I just realized my collar is turned,

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.340
so I fixed that, but I bet it was annoying to a lot of you; sorry.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.880
Right: So the most commonly cited solution to the US health care problem

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.900
is a single-payer health care system, or Medicare-for-all,

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.100
as Bernie Sanders calls it.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.340
In this proposal, all Americans would be able to

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:52.660
get the publicly-funded insurance that seniors now get

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:56.020
and studies have shown that this would lower overall U.S. health care costs,

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:58.920
although probably not to the rate seen in most European countries.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.080
Still, there would be less money spent on administration and advertising

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:03.420
and also on care itself,

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:06.860
because a bigger insurance pool can negotiate prices more aggressively

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.360
and this would be, like, a huge insurance pool.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.700
But there are trade-offs:

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:14.540
Like it's accurate to say that Medicare-for-all would lower overall U.S. health care costs,

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:17.900
it's also accurate to say it would cost hundreds of thousands of jobs.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.820
And many of them would be good-paying jobs:

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:21.260
From medical device sales people to

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:23.720
insurance adjusted to marketing managers.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:25.980
And with less money being spent per procedure,

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:27.940
lots of other people would see their incomes go down,

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:29.580
including many doctors and nurses.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:33.100
Also, it would be good to spend twelve percent of our GDP on health care

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:34.580
instead of eighteen percent

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:35.900
if we could get similar outcomes,

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.080
but there is a word for what happens

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.100
when six percent of your total economy goes away overnight:

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:42.040
Recession.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:45.200
Now, the economy would recover and reallocate capital fairly quickly,

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:46.760
but it wouldn't be painless.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:50.040
It's also important to note that we currently have a single-payer health care system

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:53.160
in the United States: in Medicare, and it's not a bargain.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:55.180
Compared to other single-payer systems around the world,

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:56.500
it's very expensive

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:00.080
because we resist the kind of government regulations and price controls

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:02.820
that are ultimately necessary to rein in spending.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.620
So a single-payer system on its own

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.340
would not solve all of our problems

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:07.480
and there would be trade-offs.

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:09.160
Alternative, some on the right have argued

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:11.620
that to increase competition and let market forces work,

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.660
it's necessary to dramatically scale back

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:16.360
or even eliminate government funding for health care.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:17.920
And that, too, might lower prices,

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:20.900
but at the cost of many millions of people losing insurance.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:23.420
Similarly, decreasing regulation might lower prices,

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:25.520
but it increases the risks of being treated

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:27.440
by an inadequately trained professional

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:29.280
or taking an unsafe medication:

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:30.260
Trade-offs.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:31.700
We can talk about over-regulation

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:32.680
or tort reform

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:34.080
or market places or competition,

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:36.740
but none of them will be a magic bullet.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:39.320
Because there is no magical solution with our health care system

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:41.580
where everyone wins and no one loses.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.500
Now, we can continue to make incremental changes,

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.720
like the ACA or alternatives to it,

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:47.160
but as Dr. Carol put it on Twitter,

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.100
however you want to reform healthcare, there will be tradeoffs.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.560
Those who promise you everything are lying.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:55.020
If we want a better health care system in the United States,

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:57.440
we need to talk openly about those trade-offs.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:59.580
Because when we allow ourselves to be pandered to,

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:02.760
our elected leaders seem more than happy to oblige us.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:04.880
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

